<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Themed-icon layer (Android 13+). The launcher recolours this to whatever the user's
    wallpaper theme is, so it must be a SINGLE-COLOUR silhouette — every shape here is
    white and the tint is applied over the top.

    ## Why this file exists at all
    Without a <monochrome> layer, a themed launcher does not fall back gracefully: it
    either leaves this app as the one full-colour tile on an otherwise themed home
    screen, or it flattens the adaptive foreground itself and produces a solid blob
    where the chevron and the bar merge — because in ic_launcher_foreground.xml those
    two shapes are distinguished ONLY by colour.

    That is the whole design note. The mark separates the scroll from the block using
    cobalt-soft against coral; a monochrome treatment throws that away, so this version
    re-states the same idea using GEOMETRY instead: the bar keeps a gap under the
    chevron and the chevron stays a stroke rather than a fill, so the two read as two
    shapes even in one colour. It is the same mark, not a different one — a themed icon
    that looks like another app's is worse than no themed icon.

    Note the app does NOT use dynamicColor anywhere in its own UI (D58 deleted the
    parameter outright, because deriving the palette from a wallpaper is exactly what a
    brand should not do). This is not a contradiction: the launcher grid is the user's
    surface and theming it is their call, whereas the app's interior is ours.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <path
        android:pathData="M54,30 L54,56"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="9"
        android:strokeLineCap="round" />
    <path
        android:pathData="M38,44 L54,60 L70,44"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="9"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round" />
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M33,71h42a6,6 0 0,1 0,12h-42a6,6 0 0,1 0,-12z" />

</vector>
